// graph.mn — Graph machinery (spec 00)
//
// The substrate. One live graph, all variables. Paged storage,
// O(1) chase amortized, epoch-monotonic, trail-backed for rollback.
// Every TVar(id) is a handle into this graph; every observer chases
// via graph_chase; only inference writes.
//
// types.mn owns the vocabulary (NodeKind, GNode, Graph, Mutation,
// GraphRead, GraphWrite). This file owns the mechanism:
// graph_handler (the live state), the SpinePage record (the paged
// storage), chase_node (the read primitive), occurs_in (cycle
// detection), the revert walkers (speculative rollback).
//
// THE PAGED SPINE. A handle's whole row — its union-find cell, its
// AST body, its attached prose, its e-class edge, its narrowing, its
// executable boundary — lives in ONE SpinePage record: band =
// handle / spine_slots, slot = handle % spine_slots. Pages allocate
// WHOLE at band-open (all six columns) and NEVER MOVE — the only
// mutation is an in-place cell write, so a checkpoint carries no
// spine copies: the open-band count is the whole guard, and
// rollback restores the COUNT. Cells and band-table slots
// above a restored count are stale and get overwritten at the next
// mint/band-open — the trail's own logical-length discipline, one
// level up. This shape deletes the doubling copy (the old
// graph_extend_to), the NFree gap-fill, and the seventeen-field
// checkpoint snapshot that moving spines forced: a page that never
// relocates cannot dangle, so the fork-spine class (the boundaries[23]
// stale-spine trap) is unconstructible, and a snapshot of the band
// table stays valid across mints.
//
// The trail keeps the flat-buffer shape — append O(1) amortized,
// rollback O(M) exact, entries above trail_len stale-and-overwritten.
// Mentl's oracle (spec 09) depends on that O(M) guarantee to explore
// hundreds of candidate patches per second.
//
// The per-module overlay family is DELETED (2026-07-25): graph_fork
// never performed anywhere in the tree, so the module partition never
// existed at runtime — every mint registered into one "global" row
// feeding a filter nobody called. Module-of-position reads LIVE through
// NModule span containment (module_path_of_span below), never a
// per-mint side-ledger.
import types

import effects

import runtime/strings

// graph_handler — the substrate handler
// State:
//   spine               — band table: SpinePage records, one per open band
//   spine_open          — count of open bands (cells above are stale)
//   trail               — flat mutation buffer (append forward, walk back on rollback)
//   trail_len           — logical length of trail (entries above are stale)
//   epoch               — bumps on every bind; query observers key (handle, epoch)
//   next                — next fresh handle to allocate
//
// Threaded through the pipeline via ~>. Installed once at compile
// entry. Every downstream pass (infer, lower, query, teach, emit,
// mentl) sees the same live graph through the same effect discipline.
//
// This is the canonical demonstration of Mentl's handler-state pattern:
// state is internal to the handler; callers never see Graph as
// a value; access is only through perform.
//
// Reverse projection over the comment-ref pairs: which comment owners
// reference this node. Linear over the pair list — the pair count is the
// program's authored cross-references, small by construction.
fn comment_ref_owners(ref pairs, referent, i, acc) with Memory + Alloc = if i >= len(pairs) {
  acc
} else {
  match list_index(pairs, i) {
    (o, r) => comment_ref_owners(pairs, referent, i + 1, if r == referent { acc ++ [o] } else { acc }),
  }
}

// ─── The paged spine — six columns, one page record per band ─────────────
// Column absent-contracts (a virgin cell is word 0 — the allocator's
// virginity contract IS the column's): the nodes column is MINT-DENSE
// (every handle below the mint bound was written at its own mint, so reads
// guard by the bound and never see a virgin cell); the program and comments
// columns guard the cell's word face (addr(cell) == 0 — the Cast
// vocabulary's read) and answer the absent value; the canon and narrowing
// columns read 0 as "no edge"; a boundaries cell's 0 IS NoBoundary (nullary
// tag 0) natively. The page is a STRUCTURAL record — mechanism, not a
// branded domain value — so field access unifies (nominal TName refuses the
// open-row field demand), and spine_page's return annotation is the shape's
// one home.
//
// One truth for the band width. 16384 slots sizes the page to the measured
// mint distribution (max 2,305 mints per decl on the wheel, p99 331 — 7×
// headroom for the per-decl banding this page structure carries next).
let spine_slots = 16384

fn spine_band(h) = h / spine_slots

fn spine_slot(h) = h % spine_slots

// The one page read — callers guarantee the band is open (mint-density for
// nodes; a spine_open guard for the sparse columns). A closed-band index
// beyond the table is the loud OOB trap, never a silent default. The return
// annotation is the Intent Boundary that CLOSES the record: an inferred open
// {nodes, ...} receiver computes field offsets over the partial demanded set
// (the trecordopen-wrong-field class — measured here as `.nodes` reading
// offset 0, the boundaries column, before the annotation).
fn spine_page(ref spine, h) -> {boundaries: [ExecutableBoundary], canon: [Int], comments: [(String, Span)], narrowing: [Int], nodes: [GNode], program: [NodeBody], spans: [Span]} with Memory =
  list_index(spine, spine_band(h))

// Open bands through band b — the MINT path only: every other write lands
// at an already-minted handle, whose band is open by construction. Returns
// the (possibly grown) table and the new open count; the table grows by
// list_extend_to (rare — once per 16384 mints), the pages themselves never
// move.
fn spine_ensure(own spine, spine_open, b) with Memory + Alloc = if b < spine_open {
  (spine, spine_open)
} else {
  spine_open_loop(list_extend_to(spine, b + 1), spine_open, b)
}

fn spine_open_loop(own spine, opened, b) with Memory + Alloc = if opened > b {
  (spine, opened)
} else {
  let page =
    {boundaries: make_list(spine_slots), canon: make_list(spine_slots), comments: make_list(spine_slots), narrowing: make_list(spine_slots), nodes: make_list(spine_slots), program: make_list(spine_slots), spans: make_list(spine_slots)}
  spine_open_loop(list_set(spine, opened, page), opened + 1, b)
}

// In-place cell writes — the page never moves, so no write returns a new
// spine and no handler-state rebind rides a cell store.
fn spine_put_node(ref spine, h, own node) with Memory = {
  let _ = list_set(spine_page(spine, h).nodes, spine_slot(h), node)
  ()
}

fn spine_put_body(ref spine, h, own body) with Memory = {
  let _ = list_set(spine_page(spine, h).program, spine_slot(h), body)
  ()
}

fn spine_put_comment(ref spine, h, own cell) with Memory = {
  let _ = list_set(spine_page(spine, h).comments, spine_slot(h), cell)
  ()
}

fn spine_put_canon(ref spine, h, to) with Memory = {
  let _ = list_set(spine_page(spine, h).canon, spine_slot(h), to)
  ()
}

fn spine_put_narrowing(ref spine, h, pred) with Memory = {
  let _ = list_set(spine_page(spine, h).narrowing, spine_slot(h), pred)
  ()
}

fn spine_put_boundary(ref spine, h, own boundary) with Memory = {
  let _ = list_set(spine_page(spine, h).boundaries, spine_slot(h), boundary)
  ()
}

fn spine_put_span(ref spine, h, own span) with Memory = {
  let _ = list_set(spine_page(spine, h).spans, spine_slot(h), span)
  ()
}

// The sparse-column reads, each answering its absent-contract for a closed
// band or a virgin cell.
fn spine_body_at(ref spine, spine_open, h) with Memory + Cast = if spine_band(h) >= spine_open {
  NHole(0)
} else {
  let cell = list_index(spine_page(spine, h).program, spine_slot(h))
  if addr(cell) == 0 { NHole(0) } else { cell }
}

// The birth-span column read — a virgin cell (never parse-indexed) is
// span_zero, the same absent-contract every position consumer already
// treats as "no source site".
fn spine_span_at(ref spine, spine_open, h) with Memory + Cast = if spine_band(h) >= spine_open {
  span_zero()
} else {
  let cell = list_index(spine_page(spine, h).spans, spine_slot(h))
  if addr(cell) == 0 { span_zero() } else { cell }
}

fn spine_comment_at(ref spine, spine_open, h) with Memory + Cast = if spine_band(h) >= spine_open {
  ("", span_zero())
} else {
  let cell = list_index(spine_page(spine, h).comments, spine_slot(h))
  if addr(cell) == 0 { ("", span_zero()) } else { cell }
}

fn spine_canon_at(ref spine, spine_open, h) with Memory = if spine_band(h) >= spine_open {
  h
} else {
  let to = list_index(spine_page(spine, h).canon, spine_slot(h))
  if to == 0 { h } else { to }
}

fn spine_narrowing_at(ref spine, spine_open, h) with Memory = if spine_band(h) >= spine_open {
  0
} else {
  list_index(spine_page(spine, h).narrowing, spine_slot(h))
}

fn spine_boundary_at(ref spine, spine_open, h) with Memory = if spine_band(h) >= spine_open {
  NoBoundary
} else {
  list_index(spine_page(spine, h).boundaries, spine_slot(h))
}

// The op-based face of the state-passed occurs family — for bracketed
// pre-check sites (unify's typed-render occurs report), which hold no
// spine: one seed read, then a zero-op walk. The arms call the family
// directly with their own state and never come here.
fn occurs_in_live(handle, ref ty) with GraphRead + Memory + Alloc + Cast + !Mutate = match graph_branch_seed() {
  (spine, _open, _spans) => occurs_in(spine, graph_next(), handle, ty),
}

// The write guard's own line — coordinates only, zero graph or intern
// reads, because the arm that speaks it cannot see its own instance (an
// arm's performs resolve OUTER). A real infinite type still deserves its
// span (SYNTAX principle 5); the typed render lives at unify's pre-check.
fn occurs_guard_line(handle, reason) with Memory + Alloc =
  "infer: E_OccursCheck error: infinite type at t{handle} (the graph write guard) at {show_span(reason_span_or_zero(reason))}\n"

// Config: a BRANCH CURSOR (the parallel fan's per-thread instance) installs
// over the SHARED spine with a private planned range — the root install is
// bare and the defaults are the empty graph. Handler state records live in
// shared heap; the install chain stays per-instance, correctly private.
handler graph_handler(spine0, spine_open0, next0, limit0, span_index0, high0, high_limit0) with spine = spine0, spine_open = spine_open0, trail = [], trail_len = 0, epoch = 0, next = next0, mint_limit = limit0, mint_high = high0, high_limit = high_limit0, span_index = span_index0, checkpoint_stack = [], comment_refs = [] {
  graph_chase(handle) => // checkpoint_stack tracks active speculation depth. Each
  // graph_push_checkpoint pushes the current trail_len; each
  // graph_rollback pops. When the stack is empty, writes are at the
  // OUTERMOST commit boundary — perform graph_mutated so the IC fixpoint
  // (the oracle's project_queue_merger re-projects, src/oracle.mn) bumps
  // its epoch only on real graph state changes. Speculative writes
  // (Mentl's oracle exploration between push+rollback) leave the stack
  // non-empty and never emit graph_mutated — otherwise infinite recursion
  // (Mentl would subscribe to her own speculations).
  //
  // ─── Read ops ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  {
    resume(chase_node(spine, next, handle, 0))
  },
  graph_chase_handle(handle) => resume(chase_handle(spine, next, handle, 0)),
  graph_epoch() => resume(epoch),
  graph_next() => resume(next),
  graph_reason_edge(h1, h2) => match chase_node(spine, next, h1, 0) {
    GNode(_, r1) => match chase_node(spine, next, h2, 0) {
      GNode(_, r2) => resume(Unified(r1, r2)),
    },
  },
  graph_reason_at(handle) => match graph_node_at(spine, next, handle) {
    GNode(_, r) => resume(r),
  },
  graph_snapshot() => resume(Graph(next, span_index)),
  graph_node_body(handle) => resume(spine_body_at(spine, spine_open, handle)),
  graph_span_of(handle) => resume(spine_span_at(spine, spine_open, handle)),
  graph_push_checkpoint() => // The checkpoint is the fork's STATE VALUE — but pages never move, so the
  // spine contributes two words (the band-table pointer, for the rare
  // in-fork table growth, and the open count) instead of six spine copies.
  // The append-only logs (trail, span_index, comment_refs) restore
  // wholesale exactly as before; per-cell truth restores by
  // walking the trail backward IN PLACE (pages are stable, so every inverse
  // lands on its live cell — the boundaries[23] stale-spine trap is
  // unconstructible in this representation). The record allocates BEFORE
  // the fork pair's heap_mark, so it survives the region.
  {
    resume(trail_len) with checkpoint_stack = push(
      checkpoint_stack,
      {comment_refs: comment_refs, next: next, span_index: span_index, spine: spine, spine_open: spine_open, trail: trail, trail_len: trail_len}
    )
  },
  graph_fresh_ty(reason) => // ─── Write ops ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // Each write appends a Mutation to the trail. The trail is the
  // inverse log — on rollback we walk it backwards.
  //
  // A mint at a planned range's ceiling jumps to the instance's own
  // overflow target (mint_high) — for the root, the open space above
  // the plan; for a BRANCH cursor, its private banded slice of it
  // (high_limit carries the band's ceiling; 0 = unlimited, the
  // Option-niche word, the slot_present precedent). The jump CONSUMES
  // the target, so a mint past the band meets mint_high 0 — an
  // UNSEEDED-or-EXHAUSTED instance, and minting would place handle 0
  // over the shared Module root's cell — the guard traps loudly.
  {
    let overflow = mint_limit > 0 && next >= mint_limit
    if overflow && mint_high == 0 { load_i32(0 - 1) } else { 0 }
    let handle = if overflow { mint_high } else { next }
    match spine_ensure(spine, spine_open, spine_band(handle)) {
      (sp, so) => {
        spine_put_node(sp, handle, GNode(NFree(epoch), reason))
        let new_trail = trail_append(trail, trail_len, MFreshNode(handle))
        if len(checkpoint_stack) == 0 { graph_mutated(epoch, MFreshNode(handle)) }
        // The jump takes the BANDED ceiling (high_limit; 0 = the root's
        // unlimited open space) and CONSUMES the target — a second
        // overflow then meets mint_high 0 and the unseeded guard traps
        // loudly instead of invading the next stmt's band.
        resume(handle) with spine = sp, spine_open = so, next = handle + 1, mint_limit = if overflow { high_limit } else { mint_limit }, mint_high = if overflow { 0 } else { mint_high }, trail = new_trail, trail_len = trail_len + 1
      },
    }
  },
  graph_fresh_row(reason) => {
    let overflow = mint_limit > 0 && next >= mint_limit
    if overflow && mint_high == 0 { load_i32(0 - 1) } else { 0 }
    let handle = if overflow { mint_high } else { next }
    match spine_ensure(spine, spine_open, spine_band(handle)) {
      (sp, so) => {
        spine_put_node(sp, handle, GNode(NRowFree(epoch), reason))
        let new_trail = trail_append(trail, trail_len, MFreshNode(handle))
        if len(checkpoint_stack) == 0 { graph_mutated(epoch, MFreshNode(handle)) }
        // The jump takes the BANDED ceiling (high_limit; 0 = the root's
        // unlimited open space) and CONSUMES the target — a second
        // overflow then meets mint_high 0 and the unseeded guard traps
        // loudly instead of invading the next stmt's band.
        resume(handle) with spine = sp, spine_open = so, next = handle + 1, mint_limit = if overflow { high_limit } else { mint_limit }, mint_high = if overflow { 0 } else { mint_high }, trail = new_trail, trail_len = trail_len + 1
      },
    }
  },
  graph_mint_plan(top) => // ─── The planned mint — the deterministic handle partition ─────────
  // The final pass pre-assigns each stmt a dense range sized by the
  // trial's measured counts (types.mn's op doc carries the contract).
  // plan(top) seeds the overflow target above every planned base; each
  // at(base, limit) raises the high-water past the range it leaves so an
  // overflow never lands inside any planned range; seal() resumes
  // sequential minting at the high-water mark. None of the three touch
  // the trail: the plan is numbering, not mutation — rollback semantics
  // ride `next` exactly as before.
  //
  // Pre-open every band the plan will touch, HERE, sequentially — a
  // branch cursor then only writes cells, never opens a band, so the
  // fan's page opens cannot race by construction (two cursors whose
  // ranges share a band boundary would otherwise both allocate it and
  // one page would leak with writes lost).
  match spine_ensure(spine, spine_open, spine_band(top - 1)) {
    (sp, so) => resume() with spine = sp, spine_open = so, mint_high = if top > mint_high { top } else { mint_high },
  },
  graph_mint_at(base, limit) => resume() with next = base, mint_limit = limit, mint_high = if next > mint_high { next } else { mint_high },
  graph_mint_seal(walk_high) => {
    // The walk's threaded frontier joins the max: branch instances'
    // overflow mints live above the plan in THEIR state — invisible
    // here — and sealing below them would hand post-seal mints the
    // same handles.
    let own_high = if next > mint_high { next } else { mint_high }
    let high = if walk_high > own_high { walk_high } else { own_high }
    resume() with next = high, mint_limit = 0, mint_high = high
  },
  graph_register_node(handle, body) => // Edge-link a node's body into the one graph at its handle — the program
  // COLUMN of the handle's own SpinePage. Every AST node registers here at
  // construction, so any handle resolves to its body live (graph_node_body)
  // — the incremental cursor keys per-module recomputation on the driver's
  // tree scan and warm buckets, never a graph-side ledger. The
  // parse-written program projection, distinct from the mutable
  // union-find type cell in the same page's nodes column — one handle, one
  // page, six columns. In-place cell write: no trail, no state rebind.
  {
    spine_put_body(spine, handle, body)
    resume()
  },
  graph_attach_comment(handle, text, span) => // The comment column — parse-attached prose in the handle's own page. A
  // comment is graph content (SYNTAX.md Comments): the parser attaches each
  // contiguous // block to the handle of the decl it precedes; the Why
  // engine / formatter / cursor project it live (graph_comment_at). A
  // SEPARATE column, not the node's single Reason slot: graph_bind
  // overwrites that slot at infer with the inference Reason, so a
  // parse-written comment Reason would be clobbered.
  //
  // Blank-separated comment blocks share a following declaration: the
  // nested arm attaches the LATER block first, so an existing entry
  // merges BEHIND the incoming earlier text — source order, one home,
  // the blank line surviving as the paragraph break.
  match spine_comment_at(spine, spine_open, handle) {
    (existing, _) => {
      let merged = if byte_len(existing) > 0 { "{text}\n\n{existing}" } else { text }
      spine_put_comment(spine, handle, (merged, span))
      resume()
    },
  },
  graph_comment_at(handle) => // A virgin cell reads as ("", zero span) — the same absent-contract every
  // column read shares.
  match spine_comment_at(spine, spine_open, handle) {
    (text, _) => resume(text),
  },
  graph_comment_span_at(handle) => match spine_comment_at(spine, spine_open, handle) {
    (_, span) => resume(span),
  },
  graph_clear_comment(handle) => // The take-side of a re-home: restore the cell to the column's own
  // absent form. A separate op because attach MERGES behind existing
  // text (the blank-separated-blocks rule above) — an empty attach
  // would prepend an empty paragraph, never clear.
  {
    spine_put_comment(spine, handle, ("", span_zero()))
    resume()
  },
  graph_attach_comment_ref(owner, referent) => // The comment-REFERENCE edges: a resolved backticked reference is a
  // real edge (owner comment's node -> referent decl's node), drawn at
  // the infer tail once the env is whole. Comments heal under rename
  // because the edge carries the identity and the NAME is a projection;
  // the audit's referenced-by line is the reverse read.
  {
    resume() with comment_refs = comment_refs ++ [(owner, referent)]
  },
  graph_comment_refs_of(referent) => resume(comment_ref_owners(comment_refs, referent, 0, [])),
  graph_canon_set(from, to) => // The e-graph equivalence weave — union-find as a fabric EDGE, a column of
  // the handle's own page beside the program/comments/nodes columns (the TVar
  // union-find precedent). `graph_canon_set(from, to)` draws the equivalence edge `from ≡
  // to`; `graph_canon_at(handle)` reads the one-step pointer (the node's
  // e-class root pointer, or the handle itself if it is its own root). The
  // rule that records the pair guarantees `to` is the cheaper node, so
  // chasing the edge always descends toward the simplest form — egraph_extract
  // (egraph.mn) follows it live. No side-ledger: an already-rooted `from`
  // overwrites its one cell, so saturation never grows an O(N²) pair-list (the
  // OOM the snoc-list ledger caused). No trail: saturation is a `|>` stage
  // between infer and lower, before speculation, so rollback never touches it.
  {
    spine_put_canon(spine, from, to)
    resume()
  },
  graph_canon_at(handle) => // A handle with no edge (absent, or a self-pointer) is its own e-class root —
  // the extracted term. A virgin cell holds 0, and handle 0 is the synthetic
  // Module root (never an expr operand), so a 0 cell reads as "no edge" ⇒
  // return the handle itself.
  {
    resume(spine_canon_at(spine, spine_open, handle))
  },
  graph_narrow_set(handle, pred_handle) => // The refinement-narrowing column — in the handle's page beside canon.
  // graph_narrow_set(handle, pred_handle) records that the path-predicate at
  // bool-node `pred_handle` is proven to hold AT program-point `handle` (§4⑥).
  // graph_narrow_at reads it back — 0 = no narrowing proven (the default for
  // every handle, so an un-narrowed program is byte-identical: lower elides
  // nothing). One cell per handle (idempotent), no side-ledger, no trail — the
  // narrowing is a `|>` stage at infer, read by lower's verify-obligation.
  {
    spine_put_narrowing(spine, handle, pred_handle)
    resume()
  },
  graph_narrow_at(handle) => resume(spine_narrowing_at(spine, spine_open, handle)),
  graph_boundary_at(site_handle) => // The executable-boundary weave: one O(1) read per fanout site, containing
  // its ordered branch edges. Unlike parse-only program/comments and post-
  // infer canon/narrowing, inference writes this weave while the oracle may be
  // under a checkpoint, so every replacement is trailed and rolls back with
  // the type nodes it names.
  {
    resume(spine_boundary_at(spine, spine_open, site_handle))
  },
  graph_boundary_set(site_handle, boundary) => {
    let old_boundary = spine_boundary_at(spine, spine_open, site_handle)
    spine_put_boundary(spine, site_handle, boundary)
    let mutation = MSetBoundary(site_handle, old_boundary)
    let new_trail = trail_append(trail, trail_len, mutation)
    let new_epoch = epoch + 1
    if len(checkpoint_stack) == 0 { graph_mutated(new_epoch, mutation) }
    resume() with epoch = new_epoch, trail = new_trail, trail_len = trail_len + 1
  },
  graph_bind(handle, ty, reason) => // The guard reads THIS instance's state DIRECTLY: the occurs family is
  // mechanism-layer (state-passed, zero ops), so the check can neither
  // chase a wrong instance (an arm's performs resolve OUTER of its own
  // install — the class that made the write guard read whatever graph
  // the enclosing chain held) nor charge GraphRead into this handler's
  // residual (the row leak that carried the render/guard ops past every
  // bracket into the wheel's own root row).
  if occurs_in(spine, next, handle, ty) {
    // The write guard's report renders NO types: an arm's performs
    // resolve OUTER of its own install, so a diag_message render here
    // would chase a graph that is not in this arm's world (the
    // wrong-instance class). The line carries coordinates only; the
    // typed teaching render lives at unify's pre-check sites (the
    // perform side, inside the analysis brackets), which fire first on
    // every unification path — this belt guards the non-unify binds.
    diag_report(
      EOccursCheck(handle, ty, reason_span_or_zero(reason)),
      occurs_guard_line(handle, reason)
    )
    resume()
  } else {
    let old_node = graph_node_at(spine, next, handle)
    spine_put_node(spine, handle, GNode(NBound(ty), reason))
    let new_trail = trail_append(trail, trail_len, MSetNode(handle, old_node))
    let new_epoch = epoch + 1
    if len(checkpoint_stack) == 0 { graph_mutated(new_epoch, MSetNode(handle, old_node)) }
    resume() with epoch = new_epoch, trail = new_trail, trail_len = trail_len + 1
  },
  graph_bind_hole(handle, reason) => // The productive-under-error poison write (infer.mn:24's law, given its
  // graph op): the node becomes an OBSERVED FAILURE. No occurs check — a hole
  // carries no type to cycle through. Trail-backed like every write, so a
  // speculative checkpoint rolls a poison back with everything else.
  {
    let old_node = graph_node_at(spine, next, handle)
    spine_put_node(spine, handle, GNode(NErrorHole(reason), reason))
    let new_trail = trail_append(trail, trail_len, MSetNode(handle, old_node))
    let new_epoch = epoch + 1
    if len(checkpoint_stack) == 0 { graph_mutated(new_epoch, MSetNode(handle, old_node)) } else { () }
    resume() with epoch = new_epoch, trail = new_trail, trail_len = trail_len + 1
  },
  graph_bind_row(handle, row, reason) => // A row is a SET, so a row var referencing itself (v = names ∪ v) is
  // idempotent — the least fixed point is `names`, v contributes nothing new,
  // and there is no infinite row (the effect universe is finite). Binding the
  // growing self-reference is what spins subst_row/subst_ty forever (the
  // result()->r cycle: r's Iterate payload names r's own row). Union-find never
  // unions a root with itself; the row sort is no different — leave the tail
  // free, resolve_row folds the names forward. NOT reported: unlike a type
  // occurs (a real infinite type), a row self-loop is benign set-idempotence.
  // The guard reads THIS instance's state directly (the graph_bind arm's
  // law): state-passed, zero ops, no wrong instance, no residual charge.
  if occurs_in_row(spine, next, handle, row) {
    resume()
  } else {
    let old_node = graph_node_at(spine, next, handle)
    // THE TEACHING WRITE IS A JOIN. A row cell is a join-semilattice
    // (present sets grow by union — the SET law above at the write): a
    // bind on an already-bound cell takes union_row(stored, incoming),
    // never a replace. Joins commute and idempote, so N caller branches
    // teaching one shared cell converge to the SAME value in ANY order —
    // the LVar/CALM law, and the death of the window-8 rebind race (the
    // born-reason census caught one cell serially re-taught five deep,
    // the surviving value whichever branch ran last; last-writer-wins
    // was also QUIETLY LOSING earlier callers' contributions in the
    // sequential walk, absorbed only by the rounds re-deriving). The
    // decl-exit REPLACE is a different operation with its own op
    // (graph_finalize_row below); the freeze that makes lattice reads
    // sound is the infer→lower phase boundary, already structural.
    let stored = match old_node {
      GNode(NRowBound(prev), r0) => {
        if row_frag_collision(prev, row) {
          // The census line speaks SPANS only (pure projections): a
          // show_reason here would perform GraphRead + Intern from the
          // arm — ops that resolve OUTER and ride this handler's
          // residual past every bracket (the face-pollution root the
          // root-row gate refused: the wheel's own main carrying
          // GraphRead + Intern it never performs).
          eprint_string("FRAGX {int_to_str(handle)} born-at {show_span(reason_span_or_zero(r0))} why-at {show_span(reason_span_or_zero(reason))}\n")
        } else {
          ()
        }
        row_join(prev, row)
      },
      _ => row,
    }
    spine_put_node(spine, handle, GNode(NRowBound(stored), reason))
    let new_trail = trail_append(trail, trail_len, MSetRow(handle, old_node))
    let new_epoch = epoch + 1
    if len(checkpoint_stack) == 0 { graph_mutated(new_epoch, MSetRow(handle, old_node)) }
    resume() with epoch = new_epoch, trail = new_trail, trail_len = trail_len + 1
  },
  graph_finalize_row(handle, row, reason) => // The decl-exit REPLACE — inf_exit_fn publishing the frame's accumulated
  // row as the fn's verdict. Not a join: the frame's accumulation is the
  // decl's own total, already containing everything the body taught, and
  // joining it with the placeholder's interim content would re-widen what
  // row_without_self deliberately stripped. One writer per cell for this
  // op (each decl finalizes its own row_handle, in its own branch).
  {
    let old_node = graph_node_at(spine, next, handle)
    spine_put_node(spine, handle, GNode(NRowBound(row), reason))
    let new_trail = trail_append(trail, trail_len, MSetRow(handle, old_node))
    let new_epoch = epoch + 1
    if len(checkpoint_stack) == 0 { graph_mutated(new_epoch, MSetRow(handle, old_node)) }
    resume() with epoch = new_epoch, trail = new_trail, trail_len = trail_len + 1
  },
  graph_compress_row(v, row) => // Path compression at the row sort — an OPTIMIZATION write, distinct
  // from the judgment bind by OP so the policy lives where the band facts
  // live: a BRANCH cursor (mint_limit > 0) SKIPS it, because a branch
  // rebinding shared chain cells makes sibling chases schedule-dependent —
  // the measured k2 yield-floor flips at window 8 traced to exactly these
  // writes (1,463 foreign row binds, every one "row path compression") —
  // while the ROOT's sequential walks re-compress the web at full
  // strength. Skipping costs a re-walk of an uncompressed chain, never a
  // divergence. The target is NRowBound by construction (compression only
  // fires on a bound link), so no self-loop guard.
  {
    if mint_limit > 0 {
      resume()
    } else {
      let old_node = graph_node_at(spine, next, v)
      spine_put_node(spine, v, GNode(NRowBound(row), Inferred("row path compression")))
      let new_trail = trail_append(trail, trail_len, MSetRow(v, old_node))
      let new_epoch = epoch + 1
      if len(checkpoint_stack) == 0 { graph_mutated(new_epoch, MSetRow(v, old_node)) }
      resume() with epoch = new_epoch, trail = new_trail, trail_len = trail_len + 1
    }
  },
  graph_bind_record_row(handle, fields, reason) => // The record-row residual bind — a `...R` rest's remaining fields, under
  // its OWN node kind (NRecordRowBound). Binding residuals under NRowBound
  // made the row slot an untagged EffRow-or-Ty union every row walk's
  // catch-all silently crossed. The occurs guard is the TYPE-side check over
  // each field ty (a residual's self-reference is a real infinite type).
  {
    if occurs_in_record_fields(spine, next, handle, fields) {
      resume()
    } else {
      let old_node = graph_node_at(spine, next, handle)
      spine_put_node(spine, handle, GNode(NRecordRowBound(fields), reason))
      let new_trail = trail_append(trail, trail_len, MSetRow(handle, old_node))
      let new_epoch = epoch + 1
      if len(checkpoint_stack) == 0 { graph_mutated(new_epoch, MSetRow(handle, old_node)) }
      resume() with epoch = new_epoch, trail = new_trail, trail_len = trail_len + 1
    }
  },
  graph_index_span(span, handle) => // ─── Rollback — the oracle's undo ────────────────────────────────
  // Walk the trail backwards from trail_len down to target. Each
  // mutation has a precise inverse, applied IN PLACE (pages never move):
  //   MSetNode(h, old) → restore the nodes cell at h to old
  //   MSetRow(h, old)  → restore the nodes cell at h to old
  //   MSetBoundary(h, old) -> restore the boundaries cell at h
  //   MFreshNode(h)    → decrement next (un-allocate)
  // Reset trail_len = target; entries above are stale and get
  // overwritten on next append — no slicing, no allocation. Epoch is
  // NOT rolled back — it stays monotonic so query observers keyed on
  // (handle, epoch) correctly see invalidation without reordering.
  {
    // ONE write, TWO projections: the ordered (span, handle) log serves
    // the containment scans (address resolution walks span -> handle);
    // the spans spine column serves the by-handle O(1) read
    // (graph_span_of). An in-place cell store rebinds no state.
    spine_put_span(spine, handle, span)
    resume() with span_index = push(span_index, (span, handle))
  },
  graph_branch_seed() => resume((spine, spine_open, span_index)),
  graph_rollback(target_idx) => {
    let n = len(checkpoint_stack)
    if n == 0 {
      // Unpaired rollback (no snapshot on the stack): the per-slot walk
      // undoes every trailed write in place — pages are stable, so each
      // inverse lands on its live cell — and MFreshNode entries count next
      // back down. Bands stay open (cells above the restored next are
      // stale-and-overwritten at re-mint, the logical-length discipline).
      let new_next = revert_trail(spine, spine_open, trail, trail_len, target_idx, next)
      resume() with next = new_next, trail_len = target_idx
    } else {
      // Walk the CURRENT trail backward, undoing each write in place into
      // the fork-time table. Backward order makes the oldest trailed
      // old-value win per slot; an entry whose band opened only during the
      // fork is skipped (its page dies with the region). Then every
      // wholesale field — including the two spine words: the band table
      // (for the rare in-fork table growth) and the open count, which
      // closes in-fork bands by COUNT (their table cells are stale above
      // it and get overwritten at the next band-open).
      let cp = last(checkpoint_stack)
      revert_trail_into(cp.spine, cp.spine_open, trail, trail_len, target_idx)
      // drop_last, never slice: the pop runs BEFORE the caller's
      // heap_reset, so a slice node minted here dies with the
      // region and the next fork walks a zeroed cell. On the
      // always-snoc stack drop_last is the parent READ — the pop
      // allocates nothing and the surviving value is pre-fork.
      resume() with spine = cp.spine, spine_open = cp.spine_open, next = cp.next, trail = cp.trail, trail_len = target_idx, span_index = cp.span_index, comment_refs = cp.comment_refs, checkpoint_stack = drop_last(checkpoint_stack)
    }
  },
}

// The Mutate effect's no-subscriber floor. graph_handler's write arms
// perform graph_mutated at every outermost commit; subscribers are a
// handler-swap surface (the IC loop's project_queue_merger re-projects on
// the bumped epoch, MV-LSP.push streams diagnostics). One-shot compile
// paths have no subscriber — this sink acknowledges the event and
// resumes. Installed OUTSIDE graph_handler in every chain that has no
// live subscriber; the IC loop swaps it for the oracle's re-projection.
handler mutate_sink {
  graph_mutated(_epoch, _mutation) => resume(),
}

// Pure graph operations — the mechanism
// Internal machinery the handler delegates to. All pure; all
// deterministic; no effects.
//
// Follow NBound / NRowBound links until terminal. Amortized O(1) —
// chains shallow in practice, depth-limited for cycle safety.
fn chase_node(ref spine, mint_bound, handle, depth) with !Mutate = if depth > 100 {
  GNode(NErrorHole(Inferred("chase depth exceeded")), Fresh(handle))
} else {
  match graph_node_at(spine, mint_bound, handle) {
    GNode(kind, reason) => match kind {
      NBound(ty) => match ty {
        TVar(next) => chase_node(spine, mint_bound, next, depth + 1),
        _ => GNode(kind, reason),
      },
      NRowBound(row) => match row {
        EfRow(names, absents, EtVar(next)) => // The chase folds the chain under the READING LAW: each level's
        // present rides forward MINUS the levels above it masked, and the
        // absents ACCUMULATE — the discarded-absent destructure here was
        // the mask-killer at the one mechanism every row read routes
        // through (a `~>` subtraction bound mid-chain vanished from every
        // graph_chase, so published faces carried the unmasked union and
        // the root-row gate refused the wheel's own main).
        chase_node(spine, mint_bound, next, depth + 1) |> merge_chased_row(??, names, absents, next),
        _ => GNode(kind, reason),
      },
      _ => GNode(kind, reason),
    },
  }
}

// chase_handle — union-find `find`: follow the TVar chain to the
// canonical ROOT handle (the integer), not the node. unify unions the
// roots; binding a surface alias instead strands the canonical var
// free. Mirror of chase_node's NBound(TVar) arm, returning the handle.
fn chase_handle(ref spine, mint_bound, handle, depth) with !Mutate = if depth > 100 {
  handle
} else {
  match graph_node_at(spine, mint_bound, handle) {
    GNode(kind, _) => match kind {
      NBound(ty) => match ty {
        TVar(next) => chase_handle(spine, mint_bound, next, depth + 1),
        _ => handle,
      },
      NRowBound(row) => // Row aliases: unify_row links open row vars as `va → EfRow(_, _, EtVar(vb))`.
      // find follows them to the canonical root var, exactly as it follows
      // TVar — so generalize/instantiate quantify a buried row tail (the row
      // mirror of the type-var find). Type handles never reach this arm.
      match row {
        EfRow(_, _, EtVar(next)) => chase_handle(spine, mint_bound, next, depth + 1),
        _ => handle,
      },
      _ => handle,
    },
  }
}

// When chasing a var-tailed row that points to another row handle,
// fold the known names forward through the chain. free_handle is the
// handle we chased INTO — needed to reconstruct the var-tailed triple
// when the chain terminates at NRowFree (the free variable).
fn merge_chased_row(ref chased, ref names, ref absents, free_handle) with !Mutate = match chased {
  GNode(ck, cr) => match ck {
    NRowFree(_) => // Preserve the accumulated names AND masks with the free
    // variable — EfRow([Memory, IO], [W], EtVar(v)) where v → NRowFree
    // keeps both fields riding the free tail.
    if len(names) == 0 && len(absents) == 0 {
      chased
    } else {
      GNode(NRowBound(ef_make(names, absents, EtVar(free_handle))), cr)
    },
    NRowBound(inner) => GNode(NRowBound(merge_row(names, absents, inner)), cr),
    _ => chased,
  },
}

// ONE law for every tail (the reading law at the unwind): the deeper
// level's present joins MINUS this level's masks; the masks union. The
// former EtAll special case (return the deeper row whole) was
// reading-equal only when this level carried no absents — with the
// masks riding, the uniform arm is the truth for it too.
fn merge_row(ref names, ref absents, ref row) with !Mutate = match row {
  EfRow(p, a, t) => ef_make(name_set_union(names, name_set_diff(p, absents)), name_set_union(absents, a), t),
}

// The one nodes read. Every minted handle's cell was written at its own
// mint, so below the bound only PLAN GAPS are unwritten (a planned range's
// final mints running under its trial measure — the cell stays virgin);
// the word-face guard answers those and any never-minted defensive read
// with the synthesized NFree, and a live handle reads its cell directly.
fn graph_node_at(ref spine, mint_bound, idx) with Memory + Cast = if idx >= mint_bound {
  GNode(NFree(0), Fresh(idx))
} else {
  let cell = list_index(spine_page(spine, idx).nodes, spine_slot(idx))
  if addr(cell) == 0 { GNode(NFree(0), Fresh(idx)) } else { cell }
}

// Occurs check
// Before graph_bind(h, ty, r): walk ty for free handles containing h.
// If found, inference emits E_OccursCheck and refuses. No silent
// binding. No cycle except through explicit <~ feedback.
//
// Iteration uses flat-indexed loops on list_to_flat'd inputs — snoc
// inputs materialize once at entry, flat inputs pass through. Walks
// short-circuit on first hit.
//
// H6: exhaustive Ty enumeration. A wildcard arm here silently
// misses a new Ty variant's containment check — the most
// dangerous site in the compiler because a missed occurs-check
// admits infinite types. Every current Ty variant is named; any
// future variant fails to compile until its containment semantics
// are decided explicitly.
fn occurs_in(spine, mint_bound, handle, ref ty) with !Mutate = match ty {
  TInt => false,
  TFloat => false,
  TUnit => false,
  TByte => false,
  TVar(id) => // The leaf answers "does handle occur in the type GRAPH reachable from
  // here" — three tiers, each a hole the selfapply fixture found trapping
  // the compiler (f(f) spun chase_deep/subst_ty to stack exhaustion with
  // ZERO occurs reports): (1) the raw handle; (2) the ROOT (unify IS
  // union-find — the indirect cycle a ~ (b -> r) with b's root already a
  // passed a raw compare); (3) the root's BINDING, recursed — bind
  // graph_bind(a, TFun([b], r)) first (b free, passes), then close the
  // cycle with graph_bind(b, TVar(a)):
  // no handle comparison sees that cycle, only descending a's bound
  // structure does. Termination is the guarded-write invariant itself:
  // every bind runs this check, so the graph stays acyclic and the
  // recursion always bottoms out (chase_deep's depth tripwire is the
  // belt behind this suspender).
  if id == handle {
    true
  } else {
    let root = chase_handle(spine, mint_bound, id, 0)
    if root == handle {
      true
    } else {
      match chase_node(spine, mint_bound, root, 0) {
        GNode(NBound(inner), _) => occurs_in(spine, mint_bound, handle, inner),
        GNode(NRecordRowBound(fields), _) => occurs_in_fields(spine, mint_bound, handle, fields),
        _ => false,
      }
    }
  },
  TList(elem) => occurs_in(spine, mint_bound, handle, elem),
  TTuple(elems) => occurs_in_list(spine, mint_bound, handle, elems),
  TFun(params, ret, eff) => occurs_in_params(spine, mint_bound, handle, params) || occurs_in(spine, mint_bound, handle, ret) || occurs_in_row(spine, mint_bound, handle, eff),
  TName(_, args) => occurs_in_list(spine, mint_bound, handle, args),
  TRecord(fields) => occurs_in_fields(spine, mint_bound, handle, fields),
  TRecordOpen(fields, v) => if v == handle || occurs_in_fields(spine, mint_bound, handle, fields) {
    true
  } else {
    let vroot = chase_handle(spine, mint_bound, v, 0)
    if vroot == handle {
      true
    } else {
      match chase_node(spine, mint_bound, vroot, 0) {
        GNode(NRecordRowBound(rest), _) => occurs_in_fields(spine, mint_bound, handle, rest),
        _ => false,
      }
    }
  },
  TRefined(base, _) => occurs_in(spine, mint_bound, handle, base),
  TCont(input, answer, _, world) => occurs_in(spine, mint_bound, handle, input) || occurs_in(spine, mint_bound, handle, answer) || occurs_in_row(spine, mint_bound, handle, world),
  TAlias(_, inner) => occurs_in(spine, mint_bound, handle, inner),
}

fn occurs_in_params(spine, mint_bound, handle, ref params) with !Mutate = {
  let flat = list_to_flat(params)
  occurs_in_params_loop(spine, mint_bound, handle, flat, 0, len(flat))
}

fn occurs_in_params_loop(spine, mint_bound, handle, ref params, i, n) with !Mutate = if i >= n {
  false
} else {
  let ty = param_ty(list_index(params, i))
  if occurs_in(spine, mint_bound, handle, ty) {
    true
  } else {
    occurs_in_params_loop(spine, mint_bound, handle, params, i + 1, n)
  }
}

fn occurs_in_list(spine, mint_bound, handle, ref tys) with !Mutate = {
  let flat = list_to_flat(tys)
  occurs_in_list_loop(spine, mint_bound, handle, flat, 0, len(flat))
}

fn occurs_in_list_loop(spine, mint_bound, handle, ref tys, i, n) with !Mutate = if i >= n {
  false
} else {
  if occurs_in(spine, mint_bound, handle, list_index(tys, i)) {
    true
  } else {
    occurs_in_list_loop(spine, mint_bound, handle, tys, i + 1, n)
  }
}

fn occurs_in_fields(spine, mint_bound, handle, ref fields) with !Mutate = {
  let flat = list_to_flat(fields)
  occurs_in_fields_loop(spine, mint_bound, handle, flat, 0, len(flat))
}

fn occurs_in_fields_loop(spine, mint_bound, handle, ref fields, i, n) with !Mutate = if i >= n {
  false
} else {
  match list_index(fields, i) {
    (_, ty) => if occurs_in(spine, mint_bound, handle, ty) {
      true
    } else {
      occurs_in_fields_loop(spine, mint_bound, handle, fields, i + 1, n)
    },
  }
}

// The occurs-check twin of free_in_row: does `handle` appear in the row's
// parameterized-effect payload types (Iterate(a) carries a)? subst_ty freshens
// these payloads via subst_row, so the check MUST see them or a var bound to a
// type carrying itself in a row (result() -> r) escapes the check and subst_ty
// loops. One traversal shape across free_in_row / subst_row / here — they read
// the SAME structure; drift between them IS the bug this closes.
//
// ONE triple, one walk: the names on both fields plus the tail var —
// the three vars-of-a-row walks (free/occurs/subst) agree by having
// one shape to agree about.
fn occurs_in_row(spine, mint_bound, handle, ref eff) with !Mutate = match eff {
  EfRow(p, a, t) => if occurs_in_eff_names(spine, mint_bound, handle, p) {
    true
  } else {
    if occurs_in_eff_names(spine, mint_bound, handle, a) {
      true
    } else {
      match t {
        EtVar(v) => // A BOUND tail hides structure the write would embed: subst_row's
        // merge arm traverses into the binding, so the cycle guard must
        // reach the same leaves (the trio law over rows, through the tail).
        {
          let r = chase_handle(spine, mint_bound, v, 0)
          if v == handle || r == handle {
            true
          } else {
            match chase_node(spine, mint_bound, r, 0) {
              GNode(NRowBound(inner), _) => occurs_in_row(spine, mint_bound, handle, inner),
              _ => false,
            }
          }
        },
        _ => false,
      }
    }
  },
}

fn occurs_in_record_fields(spine, mint_bound, handle, ref fields) with !Mutate = if len(fields) == 0 {
  false
} else {
  match last(fields) {
    (_, ty) => occurs_in(spine, mint_bound, handle, ty) || occurs_in_record_fields(spine, mint_bound, handle, drop_last(fields)),
  }
}

fn occurs_in_eff_names(spine, mint_bound, handle, ref names) with !Mutate = {
  let flat = list_to_flat(names)
  occurs_in_eff_names_loop(spine, mint_bound, handle, flat, 0, len(flat))
}

fn occurs_in_eff_names_loop(spine, mint_bound, handle, ref names, i, n) with !Mutate = if i >= n {
  false
} else {
  let occ = match list_index(names, i) {
    ENamed(_) => false,
    EPure => false,
    EParameterized(_, args) => occurs_in_eff_args(spine, mint_bound, handle, args),
  }
  if occ { true } else { occurs_in_eff_names_loop(spine, mint_bound, handle, names, i + 1, n) }
}

fn occurs_in_eff_args(spine, mint_bound, handle, ref args) with !Mutate = {
  let flat = list_to_flat(args)
  occurs_in_eff_args_loop(spine, mint_bound, handle, flat, 0, len(flat))
}

fn occurs_in_eff_args_loop(spine, mint_bound, handle, ref args, i, n) with !Mutate = if i >= n {
  false
} else {
  let occ = match list_index(args, i) {
    EAInt(_) => false,
    EAFloat(_) => false,
    EAString(_) => false,
    EAType(ty) => occurs_in(spine, mint_bound, handle, ty),
    EANode(_) => false,
  }
  if occ { true } else { occurs_in_eff_args_loop(spine, mint_bound, handle, args, i + 1, n) }
}

// Trail buffer — flat array + length counter
// Mirrors the nodes buffer substrate (DESIGN Ch 4 Salsa 3.0 shape).
// Append via list_extend_to + list_set — O(1) amortized. Revert is
// a backward flat-array loop, one list_set per step — O(M) exact.
// Rollback resets trail_len; entries above the counter are stale
// and get overwritten on next append. No snoc, no concat, no linked
// walk. This is the substrate on which Mentl's oracle runs.
//
// The snapshot-path walker: undo the trail backward IN PLACE into the
// fork-time table. Pages never move, so every inverse lands on its live
// cell (backward order makes the oldest old-value per slot the pre-fork
// one); an entry whose band opened only during the fork is skipped by the
// open-count guard — its page dies with the region, and writing into it
// would resurrect the doomed-allocation class. MFreshNode needs no step
// here: the checkpoint restores `next` wholesale.
fn revert_trail_into(ref spine, spine_open, ref trail, current, target) with Memory = if current <= target {
  ()
} else {
  let idx = current - 1
  let mutation = trail[idx]
  match mutation {
    MSetNode(h, old) => {
      if spine_band(h) < spine_open { spine_put_node(spine, h, old) }
      revert_trail_into(spine, spine_open, trail, idx, target)
    },
    MSetRow(h, old) => {
      if spine_band(h) < spine_open { spine_put_node(spine, h, old) }
      revert_trail_into(spine, spine_open, trail, idx, target)
    },
    MSetBoundary(h, old) => {
      if spine_band(h) < spine_open { spine_put_boundary(spine, h, old) }
      revert_trail_into(spine, spine_open, trail, idx, target)
    },
    MFreshNode(_) => revert_trail_into(spine, spine_open, trail, idx, target),
  }
}

// The unpaired walker: same in-place undo (every touched band is open —
// the guard is kept uniform as the belt), with MFreshNode counting `next`
// back down.
fn revert_trail(ref spine, spine_open, ref trail, current, target, next_handle) with Memory = if current <= target {
  next_handle
} else {
  let idx = current - 1
  let mutation = trail[idx]
  match mutation {
    MSetNode(h, old) => {
      if spine_band(h) < spine_open { spine_put_node(spine, h, old) }
      revert_trail(spine, spine_open, trail, idx, target, next_handle)
    },
    MSetRow(h, old) => {
      if spine_band(h) < spine_open { spine_put_node(spine, h, old) }
      revert_trail(spine, spine_open, trail, idx, target, next_handle)
    },
    MSetBoundary(h, old) => {
      if spine_band(h) < spine_open { spine_put_boundary(spine, h, old) }
      revert_trail(spine, spine_open, trail, idx, target, next_handle)
    },
    MFreshNode(_) => revert_trail(spine, spine_open, trail, idx, target, next_handle - 1),
  }
}

fn trail_append(own trail, trail_len, own mutation) =
  list_set(list_extend_to(trail, trail_len + 1), trail_len, mutation)

// Effect-name set ops (H3.1)
// merge_row routes through `name_set_union` from effects.mn — the
// row entries are EffName values, not strings. Smart constructors
// (mk_ef_closed, mk_ef_open) enforce sort invariant at construction,
// so no boundary canonicalize is needed. The local string-set
// adapters are gone — string-set primitives still serve own.mn and
// other linear-binding consumers.
//
// ── DAG-homed here (the import-truth sweep, 2026-07-23): each fn below
// moved from a module DOWNSTREAM of its users so every user reaches it
// through a forward import edge — the manifest tells the truth.
fn node_handle(node) = match node {
  N(_, _, h) => h,
}

// The MINT span of a handle — the reverse read of the span_index weave
// (fresh_handle writes (span, handle) at every mint). A node's REASON span
// can be overwritten by a later bind (a hole's reason becomes its binder's
// "declared as ..."), so a patch target must read the weave, never the
// reason. O(n) scan at felt cadence (once per accepted action); the O(1)
// form is the handle-indexed span weave (the §5.O name-is-handle sibling).
// The module path enclosing a span: the NModule weave entry whose span
// contains this span. "" when no module wraps it (single-unit weave).
// DAG-homed here (oracle and cursor both read it; both import graph) —
// module-of-position is a LIVE projection of the NModule spans, never a
// per-mint side-ledger. The O(next) scan's O(1) destiny is the
// handle-indexed span weave (the §5.O name-is-handle sibling).
fn module_path_of_span(span) with GraphRead + !Mutate = {
  let snapshot = graph_snapshot()
  match snapshot {
    Graph(next, _spans) => scan_for_enclosing_module(span, 0, next),
  }
}

fn scan_for_enclosing_module(target, i, n) with GraphRead + !Mutate = if i >= n {
  ""
} else {
  match graph_node_body(i) {
    NModule(m) => if span_contains(m.span, target) { m.path } else { scan_for_enclosing_module(target, i + 1, n) },
    _ => scan_for_enclosing_module(target, i + 1, n),
  }
}

// The birth-span read is O(1) BY HANDLE — the spans spine column
// (§5.O layer 2 landed at this read). The scan it replaces rode the
// generic find, whose iterate FLATTENS the whole (span, handle) log per
// call: the field projection calls this once per position, and a
// wheel-scale field exhausted the 4GB image inside alloc (measured:
// alloc <- flat_raw <- iterate <- find <- parse_span_of); the
// allocation-free scan then measured O(n^2) over the snoc-spined log
// (a 600s timeout at the same scale). The column is the same one write
// projected by handle — the log survives for containment scans.
fn parse_span_of(handle) with GraphRead = graph_span_of(handle)

// The ONLY route from a TypeHandle to a resolved Ty. Delegates to
// graph_chase. No cache. No staleness. Every observer reads live.
// The effect decl beside its ONE handler (the order-independence law).
effect LookupTy {
  lookup_ty(Int) -> Ty
  // The specialization bracket (named-generic twins): while set, the handler
  // answers a free var through the site's EXACT instantiation pairs (root
  // handle -> concrete type; sound since the subscript fracture fix made a
  // generic body's element classes one union-find root), and the
  // active enc lets the closure-mint arm redirect a nested lambda to its
  // twin record. State on the ONE LookupTy handler — never a second handler
  // (singleton tier).
  spec_ctx_set([(Int, Ty)], String)
  spec_ctx_clear()
  spec_enc_active() -> String
}

// ─── the specialization projection — lookup_ty under a twin's pairs ──
//
// The key is the repr digit vector over the scheme's quantified vars, sorted
// by root handle — tiny, and maximally shared: every word instantiation IS
// the floor emission ([Int], String, records — byte-identical, Law 7); only
// a genuinely wide var mints a twin ($min$sp2 = min with its one var at f64).
fn spec_pairs_find(pairs, root) with Memory + Alloc = match find(
  (p) => match p {
    (v, _) => v == root,
  },
  pairs
) {
  Some(p) => match p {
    (_, t) => Some(t),
  },
  None => None,
}

// Exact root-keyed substitution — the twin's one rule. Sound since the
// subscript fracture fix (Force = UNIFY at IndexExpr): a generic body's
// element classes and the scheme's quantified vars are ONE union-find root,
// so a free leaf answers its OWN root's binding in the site's instantiation
// pairs — and a free var whose root is NOT in the pairs stays the honest
// floor TVar. This is the precision the interim free-leaf rule (every free =
// the one wide type) could not have, and it lifts the one-wide-type demand
// guard: multi-type generics (a := Float, b := Int) twin correctly.
fn spec_pair_or_var(u, pairs) with GraphRead + Memory + Alloc = match spec_pairs_find(pairs, graph_chase_handle(u)) {
  Some(c) => c,
  None => TVar(u),
}

fn spec_subst_pairs(t, pairs) with GraphRead + Memory + Alloc = match t {
  TVar(u) => match graph_chase(u) {
    GNode(kind, _r) => match kind {
      NBound(t2) => spec_subst_pairs(t2, pairs),
      NFree(_) => spec_pair_or_var(u, pairs),
      NErrorHole(_) => spec_pair_or_var(u, pairs),
      NRowBound(_) => t,
      NRecordRowBound(_) => t,
      NRowFree(_) => t,
    },
  },
  TList(e) => TList(spec_subst_pairs(e, pairs)),
  TTuple(es) => TTuple(map((x) => spec_subst_pairs(x, pairs), es)),
  TFun(ps, r, eff) => TFun(
    map((p) => param_with_ty(p, spec_subst_pairs(param_ty(p), pairs)), ps),
    spec_subst_pairs(r, pairs),
    eff
  ),
  TName(n, args) => TName(n, map((x) => spec_subst_pairs(x, pairs), args)),
  TRecord(fs) => TRecord(map(
    (f) => match f {
      (n, ft) => (n, spec_subst_pairs(ft, pairs)),
    },
    fs
  )),
  TRecordOpen(fs, v) => mk_record_open(
    map(
      (f) => match f {
        (n, ft) => (n, spec_subst_pairs(ft, pairs)),
      },
      fs
    ),
    v
  ),
  TRefined(b, p) => TRefined(spec_subst_pairs(b, pairs), p),
  TAlias(n, i) => TAlias(n, spec_subst_pairs(i, pairs)),
  TCont(i, a, d, w) => TCont(spec_subst_pairs(i, pairs), spec_subst_pairs(a, pairs), d, w),
  _ => t,
}

// lookup_ty_graph — GraphRead → LookupTy bridge (spec 05)
// The ONLY LookupTy handler in Phase 1. Declares GraphRead
// (inherited from the outer graph_handler). Free and error-hole handles
// resume TVar(h) — the honest word floor every consumer reads; a width a
// site genuinely needs detonates at emit's loud floors, never here.
//
// The handler additionally carries the SPECIALIZATION state (spec_pairs): a
// named-generic twin's emission brackets itself with spec_ctx_set/clear, and
// while set, a free var answers through the site's EXACT instantiation pairs
// (spec_subst_pairs above — root-keyed; a free var whose root is not in
// the pairs stays the honest floor TVar). State-swap, NOT handler-swap:
// a second LookupTy handler would demote every lookup_ty perform out of the
// singleton direct-dispatch tier — measured as ev-scan faults at infer sites
// that never threaded evidence (they never needed to under the singleton).
handler lookup_ty_graph with spec_pairs = [], spec_enc = "" {
  spec_ctx_set(prs, e) => resume() with spec_pairs = prs, spec_enc = e,
  spec_ctx_clear() => resume() with spec_pairs = [], spec_enc = "",
  spec_enc_active() => resume(spec_enc),
  lookup_ty(h) => match graph_chase(h) {
    GNode(kind, reason) => if len(spec_pairs) > 0 {
      match kind {
        NBound(t) => resume(spec_subst_pairs(t, spec_pairs)),
        NFree(_) => resume(spec_pair_or_var(h, spec_pairs)),
        NErrorHole(_) => resume(spec_pair_or_var(h, spec_pairs)),
        NRowBound(_) => resume(TUnit),
        NRecordRowBound(_) => resume(TUnit),
        NRowFree(_) => resume(TUnit),
      }
    } else {
      match kind {
        NBound(t) => resume(t),
        NErrorHole(_) => // An error hole / free var resumes TVar(h) — CARRY THE HANDLE. The
        // prior TName("ERROR_HOLE")/TName("UNRESOLVED") sentinels fabricated
        // a NOMINAL type for a node the graph proves is unresolved: every
        // downstream type-directed read then trusted the lie — the eq
        // dispatch minted a structural-sum helper $eq_nUNRESOLVED that read
        // a raw Int operand as a variant pointer (memory at address 44100 as
        // a tag word). TVar(h) is the truth: repr_of reads the word floor,
        // fold_sig reads "i", eq reads word identity — the honest
        // productive-under-error floor at every consumer, no second sentinel
        // channel. (A quantified var's == at a GENERIC body is word identity
        // by the same floor; the type-directed eq for a generic operand at
        // its instantiated width is the evidence-passing band —
        // Hβ.emit.generic-boundary-repr-coercion.)
        resume(TVar(h)),
        NFree(_) => // A free var read at lower is NOT an error — it is a legal quantified
        // parameter of a reachable generic declaration (generalize's Forall is
        // the graph's own proof; `fn ident(x) = x` called from main carries
        // one), and repr_of floors it to the honest word by design. The per-read
        // report here re-derived "free = error" against that proof — 182
        // false diagnostics on a clean runtime-lib compile, the whole
        // frontier-gate debt class. A GENUINELY stuck width still detonates
        // at emit's loud comment-marked floors (field-offset-unprovable, the
        // ft mismatch), at the site that actually needs the width — the
        // consumer owns the refusal, never the read.
        resume(TVar(h)),
        NRowBound(_) => resume(TUnit),
        NRecordRowBound(_) => resume(TUnit),
        NRowFree(_) => resume(TUnit),
      }
    },
  },
}
